course: everyday
lesson: 450
title: Too many details, too few
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Mia asked her brother where he'd been. «Went to Jake's at 4:15, played FIFA, he won 3–2 with Arsenal, his mum made pasta with red sauce, left at 6:02.» Nobody remembers an ordinary afternoon to the minute. It sounded like a script he'd practised. Mia's mum rang Jake's mum: he hadn't been there at all. A story with too many neat details for an ordinary thing can be made up — but so can a story with too few.
explain: Noah asked his mate what he did at the weekend. «Stuff.» What stuff? «Just stuff.» Where? «Places.» Too few details can mean a lie — or a secret that hurts. Noah let it go; weeks later his mate told him his parents were splitting up. Mia's friend Lucy said she couldn't come to the sleepover because of «a family thing». Mia said «Okay, no worries» and didn't push. The next week Lucy told her she was scared of sleepovers, and Mia said «Come for tea instead, then go home.» Not every vague answer needs cracking open; sometimes leaving space lets people tell you later.
explain: Watch where the details are. A girl in Glasgow said she'd «met the King» at a garden party — loads about the cake and the hats, nothing at all about the King. Later she admitted he'd been «far away, like». Ruby's friend described her «trip to Paris» with things you'd see in any film: the Eiffel Tower, a croissant, «a man with a beret». Ruby asked «What was your hotel like?» and the friend changed the subject. Real trips have small odd details — a grumpy waiter, a rainy day, a lost sock. Theo said he'd been «at the dentist», but he had no sticker, no leaflet, no numb mouth. «What did the dentist say?» «Er… teeth are good.» He'd skipped school. Real events leave traces.
explain: Details are not always a bad sign. Ava's cousin told a story about a bear at a campsite: the smell, the scrape of claws on the bin, her dad shouting in his pants. Ava believed her — and later saw the photos. True stories often have odd details from the senses, and little mistakes. Lies made up on purpose often have neat details, all in order, like a list.
explain: Watch for words that skip the proof. A video said «Scientists PROVED this one food cures everything!» Leo's mum asked, «Which scientists? Which study?» No names, no place, no date. On the radio a man said «Everyone knows…», and Callum in Belfast asked who «everyone» was. «Good question, son,» Dad laughed — they looked it up, and it wasn't true. Ethan's mate said he couldn't come out because «my mum said, she's being weird». Later his mum said she'd said nothing; he just didn't feel like it. When a reason is pushed onto someone else, ask yourself whether it's really theirs.
fact: Most people don't remember ordinary events to the exact minute; a story that is too perfect can be a practised script.
fact: Too few details, like «stuff» and «places», can mean a lie, or a private worry the person doesn't want to share.
fact: Not every vague answer should be pushed; giving space can help someone tell you later.
fact: Notice where a story's details are rich and where they suddenly stop; the missing part may be the made-up part.
fact: A description made only of famous things everyone knows, with nothing personal, may come from films rather than memory.
fact: Real events usually leave traces, like stickers, receipts, photos or a sore mouth.
fact: True stories often have odd details from the senses — smells, sounds, feelings — and small mistakes.
fact: Made-up stories often have neat details told in perfect order.
fact: Real facts come with sources: who found them, where and when.
fact: «Everyone knows», «it's obvious» and «trust me» skip the proof; that is the moment to ask for it.
fact: People sometimes blame someone else for their own choice; «I don't feel like it» is the honest version.
fact: No single clue proves a lie; clues are reasons to ask kindly or to check.
fact: «Like» at the end of a sentence (Scotland, North England) softens it; «pants» in Britain are underpants; «mint» (UK) means great; a «bloke» is a man.
exercise: Why did Mia's brother's story sound made up? | It had too many exact details for an ordinary afternoon | It had no details at all about the afternoon, only the time he got home | He told it while laughing at Mia | He said he'd been at Jake's house
exercise: How was the brother's story checked? | Mum rang Jake's mum | Mia asked Jake's dog | They looked at the FIFA score | They asked the pasta shop
exercise: Noah's mate said «stuff» and «places». What turned out to be the reason? | His parents were splitting up | He'd robbed a shop at the weekend | He'd been on holiday abroad all week | He had forgotten everything about it
exercise: Why did Lucy really not come to the sleepover? | She was scared of sleepovers | She had a family wedding to go to that weekend | She didn't like Mia | She was ill that night
exercise: What kind thing did Mia do about Lucy's vague answer? | Didn't push, and later offered tea instead | Asked her twenty questions | Told everyone Lucy was lying | Rang Lucy's mum to check the story was really true
exercise: What was missing from the Glasgow girl's story about the King? | Any details about the King himself | Any details about the cake | Any details about the hats | Any details about the palace garden
exercise: Why did Ruby doubt the «Paris trip»? | It had only famous things from films, nothing personal | It had far too many personal details about the hotel staff | The friend showed her photos | The friend spoke French
exercise: What question made Ruby's friend change the subject? | «What was your hotel like?» | «Did you see the Eiffel Tower?» | «Did you eat a croissant?» | «Was it in France?»
exercise: Which missing trace gave away Theo's «dentist» story? | No sticker, no leaflet, no numb mouth | His teeth were far too white and shiny for a check-up | He brought a toothbrush home | The dentist rang the school
exercise: Was Ava's cousin's bear story true? | Yes — and Ava later saw the photos | No, there are no bears at campsites | No, it had too many details | Nobody ever found out
exercise: What kind of details do true stories often have? | Odd details from the senses and small mistakes | Exact times to the minute, all in the right order | Only famous places everyone knows | No details at all
exercise: What did Leo's mum ask about «scientists PROVED»? | Which scientists, which study? | How many views the video had | Whether the food tasted nice | Who made the music
exercise: What does «everyone knows» often do? | Skip the proof and stop people checking | Prove a thing is true | Show that many people have checked it carefully | Mean scientists agree
exercise: What was really behind Ethan's mate's «my mum said»? | He didn't feel like coming out | His mum had grounded him for a week | His mum was ill in bed all day | He had loads of homework to finish
exercise: What does one clue, like a story with no details, prove? | Nothing by itself — it's a reason to ask or check | That the person is definitely lying | That the person is definitely honest and has nothing to hide | That you should stop being friends
exercise: In Britain, what are «pants»? | Underpants | Trousers | Socks | Shorts
test: Bro: «got to Jake's 4:15, left 6:02, pasta red sauce, 3–2 Arsenal». Mum, dry: «Very precise.» Why is she suspicious? | Nobody remembers an afternoon to the minute | Pasta never has red sauce on a weekday | Arsenal never wins 3–2 | Jake doesn't have a house with a TV in it
test: Mate in Glasgow: «met the King, like, the cakes were mint, the hats were mad». Ask what? | What was the King like? | What flavour were the cakes? | What colour were the hats? | What time was the party?
test: «Scientists say chocolate makes you clever!!» No names, no study. What's the sharpest reply? | Which scientists, and where's the study? | Brilliant, give me chocolate | Scientists always agree on food, so it must be right | If it's said loud it's true
test: Ava's cousin's bear story has smells, sounds and her dad yelling in his pants. Your mate says «too many details = lie». What's smarter? | Sense details often come from real memories | Every detailed story is a lie, however small the details | Only short stories are true | Bears don't make sounds
test: What would happen if Mia had pushed Lucy hard about «the family thing»? | Lucy might have felt worse and not told her | Lucy would have told the truth faster and felt much better | Lucy would have come to the sleepover | Nothing would have changed
test: Radio bloke: «Everyone knows that!» Callum: «Who's everyone?» Why is that a good question? | It asks for proof instead of a crowd | It's rude to the radio man | It means Callum knows everyone who listens to the radio | Everyone really does know
test: Theo, back «from the dentist» — no sticker, normal mouth, eats a toffee straight away. What's most likely? | He wasn't at the dentist | The dentist forgot the sticker | Dentists don't touch teeth | Toffee is good after the dentist
test: Kid: «Paris was sick, Eiffel Tower, croissants, a man in a beret». «Sick» here means great. What's missing that a real trip would have? | Small personal odd details | Famous places | The word «Paris» | A croissant
test: Noah's mate just says «stuff, places» about his weekend and looks at the floor. What's wise? | Let it go kindly; he may tell you later | Call him a liar in front of everyone | Follow him next weekend to see where he really goes | Tell the teacher he's lying
test: A teacher says: «The detailed story is the true one, always.» A sharp kid would say what? | It depends — scripts can be neat, real stories messy | Yes, details always mean truth | No, details always mean lies | Details don't matter at all
test: Ethan's mate: «can't come out, me mam said, she's being weird». Later his mam says she said nothing. What was going on? | He didn't feel like it and blamed his mam | His mam forgot what she said | His mam is always weird | He was being kidnapped
test: «Trust me, it's obvious.» What's the right moment to do? | Ask for the proof | Trust him because he said so | Change the subject | Agree to avoid an argument
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an account of an ordinary afternoon recounted to the exact minute may arouse suspicion. | Ordinary recollection seldom retains such precision, suggesting a prepared script | Precise accounts are invariably truthful, a liar being unable to invent so many particulars | Ordinary afternoons do not occur | Precision is forbidden in conversation
exam: It is given that an account abounds in detail concerning peripheral matters but contains none concerning its central event. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The absence of detail concerning the central event may indicate fabrication | The account is necessarily accurate in all respects | Peripheral detail concerning cake and hats proves the central event beyond doubt | Central events are never described in detail
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an unsupported assertion that «scientists have proved» a claim? | It lacks a stated source and should be treated with caution | It constitutes conclusive scientific evidence | It is necessarily false | It is verified by its manner of presentation
exam: Determine which of the following does NOT constitute a sound conclusion concerning an evasive reply such as «stuff». | That it proves the speaker to be lying | That it may conceal a private difficulty | That it may conceal a falsehood | That giving the speaker space may be appropriate
